{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-38355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008055/assets/718dd1f38f7c790ab83639f69462b372de6b7e8501c0a4d6ff678340ab4154bd.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008055/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b4ed42ea65d91fd2df0b85f6618fca6bd2696b2444645c57ea2bc323876eb49"} {"question" "q-38355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false.  The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008055/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b4ed42ea65d91fd2df0b85f6618fca6bd2696b2444645c57ea2bc323876eb49"} {"question" "q-38355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008055/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d761c983c50a4f38a80dc31b40a48f91f79070868f6f3a212d0b95189cadba11"} {"questi